    {
      "name": "pgwhen",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/ingridh-demo/pgwhen",
      "description": "Query a Postgres database as it was at any point in time, from WAL archives.",
      "fork": false,
      "archived": false,
      "is_template": false,
      "stars": 2140,
      "forks": 178,
      "language": "Go",
      "topics": [
        "postgresql",
        "wal",
        "time-travel",
        "backup"
      ],
      "homepage": "https://pgwhen.dev",
      "license": "MIT",
      "created_at": "2021-02-11T10:00:00Z",
      "pushed_at": "2026-09-01T10:00:00Z",
      "default_branch": "main",
      "languages_pct": {
        "Go": 91.2,
        "Shell": 5.1,
        "Dockerfile": 3.7
      },
      "readme_excerpt": "# pgwhen\nPoint a read-only Postgres endpoint at any timestamp. pgwhen restores just enough WAL from your archive\n(S3, GCS, filesystem) into an ephemeral instance and proxies your queries to it.\n\n    pgwhen serve --archive s3://backups/main --at \"2026-05-03 14:02:00Z\"\n    psql \"postgres://localhost:6543/app\" -c \"select * from orders where id = 4411\"\n\n- Works with pgBackRest and WAL-G archives\n- Ephemeral instances are cached and garbage-collected\n- Used for incident forensics and \"what did this row look like yesterday?\" questions\n",
      "readme_truncated": false
    },
